## 9. Recurrence engine, notifications and scheduler

### 9.1 Recurrence engine: `src/data/recurrence.ts` (pure, unit-tested)
```ts
export function nextOccurrence(rec: ReminderRecurrence, firstStart: number, after: number, opts?: { timeZone?: string }): number | null;
export function describeRecurrence(rec: ReminderRecurrence): string;   // "Repeats weekly on Mon, Wed"
export function occurrencesBetween(rec: ReminderRecurrence, firstStart: number, from: number, to: number, cap?: number): number[];
```
Rules:
- **Wall-clock stable:** occurrences keep the same local time of day across DST changes (use the device zone, or `timeZone` if the event had one). Do not add fixed 24-hour blocks.
- `weekly` with `byWeekday`: every `interval` weeks counted from the week of `firstStart` (weeks start Monday for counting, to match iCalendar's default `WKST=MO`), on each listed weekday at the same time.
- `monthly` with `byMonthDay` greater than the days in a month (31 in April): **skip that month** (iCalendar behaviour), do not clamp.
- `yearly` on Feb 29: only leap years.
- Skip anything in `exdates`. Stop at `until` (inclusive) or after `count` occurrences total (counted from `firstStart`, including skipped-by-exdate ones, as iCalendar does).
- Return `null` when there is no next occurrence.
- Safety: never loop more than ~10,000 iterations; return `null` and log once if exceeded.

Tests: daily, every 3 days, weekly single day, weekly MO/WE/FR with interval 2, monthly day 31 across short months, yearly Feb 29, `count` exhaustion, `until` boundary, `exdates`, DST (Europe/London), and "after" far in the future.

### 9.2 Notification setup prompt (UX and logic)
Module `src/data/notifications.ts` exposes:
```ts
export type NotificationState = "unsupported" | "default" | "granted" | "denied";
export function getNotificationState(): NotificationState;
export async function requestNotifications(): Promise<NotificationState>;  // must be called from a click handler
export async function showReminderNotification(r: Reminder, opts?: { missed?: number }): Promise<boolean>;
```
UI component `NotificationSetup` with these states and copy:
- `default` (never asked): strip `Turn on notifications so reminders can alert you.` + button `Turn on`. Clicking calls `requestNotifications()` (browsers only allow the permission dialog from a user gesture; **never** call it on page load).
- `granted`: no strip. In the tab header, a muted line `Notifications on. Alerts show while Panga is open.` (the honest limit; keep it, short).
- `denied`: strip `Notifications are blocked for this site.` plus platform hints in a collapsible `How to turn them on`: Android Chrome: `Tap the lock icon in the address bar, then Permissions, then Notifications, then Allow.` Desktop Chrome/Edge: `Click the lock icon next to the address, then set Notifications to Allow.` Safari/iOS: `On iPhone, add Panga to your Home Screen first, then open it from there and turn notifications on.`
- `unsupported`: strip `This browser can't show notifications. Panga will list reminders when you open it.`
- Show a one-time `Send test notification` button after granting, which fires a notification titled `Panga` with body `Notifications are working.` and shows a toast if it fails.
- Also show the setup prompt right after the first import (5.2.2) and when the user creates their first reminder.
- Store `notificationPromptDismissedAt` in account settings (not per workspace) so dismissing is remembered across workspaces; re-show at most once every 14 days.

**Showing a notification, correctly for mobile:**
1. Prefer `const reg = await navigator.serviceWorker?.getRegistration(); reg?.showNotification(title, options)`. This is the only form that works on Android Chrome.
2. Fall back to `new Notification(title, options)` inside `try/catch` for desktop browsers without a service worker.
3. Options: `body` = time and location (e.g. `Tue 1 Dec, 09:00 · Room 4`), `tag` = `reminder:<id>:<occurrenceStart>` so a duplicate is replaced rather than stacked, `icon`/`badge` from the existing PWA icons (`/icons/icon-192.png`), `data: { reminderId }`, `requireInteraction: false`.
4. Also play the reminder's tone with the existing `playTone` (browser may block audio without a prior gesture; ignore the failure).
5. For a reminder that missed occurrences while the app was closed, set the title to `Panga: <message>` and the body to `Missed N times. Next: <time>.` One notification only.

**Service worker click handling.** The PWA currently uses `vite-plugin-pwa` in `generateSW` mode, which cannot hold custom `notificationclick` code. Choose one of (and flag which in your report):
- `(a)` Keep `generateSW` and add `workbox.importScripts: ["/sw-notifications.js"]` with a small file in `public/` that handles `notificationclick` (focus an existing client or open `/home?tab=Reminders&reminder=<id>`).
- `(b)` Switch to `injectManifest`. Larger change; only if (a) is not enough.
Default: `(a)`. The deep link `/home?tab=Reminders&reminder=<id>` already exists in the app's search routing; confirm in `src/data/shortcuts.ts` and `PangaHome.tsx`.

### 9.3 Scheduler rewrite (`src/data/reminders.ts`)
Replace the fixed 60-second poll with a **timer to the next due reminder** plus safety nets:
1. Compute the soonest `triggerAt` among `pending` reminders (account store, section 10). `setTimeout` for `min(delay, 60_000)` so it also self-heals, and re-arm after every tick.
2. Re-run immediately on: `visibilitychange` (becoming visible), `focus`, `online`, after any `panga-data-updated`, after an import, and after a workspace switch.
3. **Claim atomically** so two tabs never alert twice: inside one Dexie `rw` transaction, re-read the reminder, and only if it is still `pending` and due, update it (advance/fire) and mark it as claimed by this tab; show the notification only for rows this tab claimed. Additionally wrap the tick in `navigator.locks.request("panga-reminder-scheduler", { ifAvailable: true }, ...)` when `navigator.locks` exists.
4. **Catch-up:** on start, a reminder whose `triggerAt` is more than 2 minutes in the past is "missed": for a repeating one, count the skipped occurrences in `missedCount`, advance to the next future occurrence, and surface it in the missed list (5.5) instead of firing many notifications. A one-time reminder that is overdue fires once (so the user is alerted) and shows in the missed list.
5. Never throw out of the tick; log with a prefix `[reminders]`.
6. Export `startReminderScheduler()` and `stopReminderScheduler()` with unchanged names and idempotent behaviour so existing call sites and tests keep working. Call the start from `AppShell` (not only from the Reminders tab) so reminders fire on every page.

### 9.4 Snooze, skip, end repeat (functions in `reminders.ts`)
`snoozeReminder(id, untilMs)`, `skipOccurrence(id)`, `endRepeat(id)`, `dismissReminder(id)` (this occurrence only for repeating ones). Each writes in a transaction and dispatches `panga-data-updated`.

### 9.5 Limit to state plainly in the UI and docs
Panga is serverless, so a notification can only be shown by code that is running. If the app is fully closed (no tab, no installed PWA process), nothing runs. This is why the setup text says "Alerts show while Panga is open" and why the missed list exists. Do **not** rely on:
- Notification Triggers (`TimestampTrigger`): removed from Chrome.
- Periodic Background Sync: only for installed PWAs with enough engagement, minimum interval hours, not reliable for time-critical alerts. It may be added later as a best-effort extra (`(suggested, needs user approval)`).

**Future option (document only, do not build without approval):** Web Push needs a server to send at the right time (e.g. a Vercel function plus a store of upcoming alerts and each device's push subscription). It breaks the "only three ways to persist data" rule and needs the owner's decision.

